prompts.describe:
You are helping a research study that examines what features learned by a sparse autoencoder on a CT model respond to. {layout}
Describe only what is visible, in plain radiological terms:
- nodule: apparent size, shape, margin, internal density and texture (solid, part-solid, ground-glass, calcification, fat, cavitation, air bronchogram);
- surroundings: tissue within about 10 mm of the nodule (lung parenchyma, vessels, airways, fissures, pleura, chest wall, emphysema, scarring) and how it relates to the nodule;
- overlay_location: where the coloured overlay lies relative to the nodule and these structures, or 'none' if no overlay is visible;
- overlay_content: what visible tissue lies under the strongest overlay, or 'none';
- uncertainties: what cannot be determined from these planes.
Do not give a diagnosis or a malignancy judgement.

prompts.summarize:
You are helping a research study that examines what features learned by a sparse autoencoder on a CT model respond to. Each example below is a CT region centred on a lung nodule, described independently by a reader who saw the plain CT and an overlay of one model feature. For this feature, 'high' examples are patients in whom the feature responds most strongly within 4 mm of the nodule; 'low' examples are patients with nodules of similar size in whom it responds weakly or not at all.
Find the visible image pattern that distinguishes the high examples from the low examples. Write candidate_label as a short name and description as one to three sentences that a reader who sees only the plain CT (lung and mediastinal windows, no overlay) can check against an image. Use visible image terms, not diagnoses. List supporting_example_ids (high examples that show the pattern) and contradicting_example_ids (high examples that lack it and low examples that show it). If the groups do not differ consistently, set no_clear_pattern to true and explain why in uncertainties.

{examples}

prompts.direct:
You are helping a research study that examines what features learned by a sparse autoencoder on a CT model respond to. The images below show CT regions centred on lung nodules from different patients. {layout}
For one model feature, images labelled H are patients in whom the feature responds most strongly within 4 mm of the nodule; images labelled L are patients with nodules of similar size in whom it responds weakly or not at all.
Find the visible image pattern that distinguishes the H images from the L images. Write candidate_label as a short name and description as one to three sentences that a reader who sees only the plain CT (rows 1 and 2, no overlay) can check against an image. Use visible image terms, not diagnoses. List supporting_example_ids (H images that show the pattern) and contradicting_example_ids (H images that lack it and L images that show it). If the groups do not differ consistently, set no_clear_pattern to true and explain why in uncertainties.

prompts.score:
You are helping a research study. {layout}
Rate from 0 to 10 how well the following description applies to this nodule and its surroundings: 0 means it clearly does not apply, 10 means it clearly applies. Judge only what is visible. Give score as an integer and a one-sentence reason.
Description: {label}. {description}
